= V/ CONCLUSION AND PERSPECTIVES

This study demonstrated the fabrication, electrochemical optimization, and electrical characterization of bio-inspired PDA volatile memristors for neuromorphic applications. The Cu/C/PDA (protonated)/ITO stack was integrated into a $6 times 6$ crossbar array. While a direct Cu/PDA control was not fabricated to experimentally isolate the carbon barrier's contribution, the carbon interlayer is proposed to serve as a diffusion barrier to control copper ion migration based on comparative literature models. AFM revealed conformal film coverage with surface roughness improving from Sa = 3.35 nm (normal) to 2.683 nm (protonated), while active layer thicknesses were approximately 23 nm for both films from height distribution analysis.

Electrochemical optimization via CA at $+0.25"V"$ for $60"s"$ in acidic buffer successfully converted the PDA film to the proton-doped state. EIS analysis revealed a charge-transfer resistance increase from 1.1 kΩ (undoped) to 65.6 kΩ (proton-doped). XPS and Raman spectroscopy confirmed the structural reorganization.

Under DC characterization, the devices exhibited volatile threshold switching at approximately 0.56 V with an $R_"HRS"$/$R_"LRS"$ ratio of $10^3$. Weibull analysis yielded shape parameters $beta$ of 5.63 (device-to-device), 5.54 (sample-to-sample), and 4.36 (batch-to-batch), demonstrating highly uniform switching. Pulsed endurance exceeded $10^6$ cycles, with active transient switching energy evaluated at 0.45 nJ at 1.25 V and 2.00 nJ at 2.75 V via time-resolved numerical integration. Under 50-pulse stimulation (500 ns inter-pulse delay), the devices demonstrated pulse-train potentiation with a Synaptic Gain Index ($A_50 / A_1$) of 103.61% (+3.61% cumulative increase). Downsizing the 200 µm × 200 µm crossbar junction and optimizing pulse duration provide a clear path to scale energy consumption into the picojoule-to-femtojoule regime. Although the nanosecond volatile relaxation (213–461 ns) is consistent with Grotthuss-type protonic transit times, this timescale can also support electronic relaxation (e.g., charge detrapping). In the absence of temperature-dependent electrical measurements or the observation of discrete quantized conductance steps, these relaxation and conduction pathways cannot be definitively isolated. Nonetheless, the results suggest that the functional groups in the electrochemically protonated PDA layer enable a switching mechanism mediated by proton-coupled charge transport.

Future work should include a systematic PPF study with varying inter-pulse interval, pulse amplitude, and pulse count to fully map the short-term plasticity dynamics and establish quantitative comparison with biological synaptic facilitation @hu_emulating_2013. Temperature-dependent electrical measurements would provide deeper insight into the conduction mechanism and activation energies. In-situ spectroscopic techniques (e.g., Raman or XPS under bias) and conductive AFM measurements are needed to directly resolve the charge transport dynamics and provide definitive evidence for the proposed switching mechanism. Further investigation of the reduced control state obtained at $-0.25"V"$ potential is also required, as its transport behavior may differ from the protonated state (+0.25 V) studied here.
